const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"RC_E7_001","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P1","passage":"In The Nutmeg's Curse: Parables for a Planet in Crisis, Amitav Ghosh explores the spice nutmeg as a parable for our contemporary climate crisis, showing how human history has always been entangled with earthly materials. Offering a lucid call to action that seeks to inspire a collective response to the crisis, this book is urgent reading. In The Nutmeg's Curse, Ghosh recounts the histories of Western colonialism through the nutmeg. Yes, the spice that was originally found in the Banda Islands of Indonesia and has since travelled far and wide at the hands of trade and commerce to be present in kitchens across the world. What comes out of this fairly familiar trope of exploring a global commodity like nutmeg is a critical history of colonialism and capitalism that has been frequently told.\\n\\nIn the first chapter titled 'A Lamp Falls', we are not only introduced to the Dutch East India Company, islanders' resistance to it and the subsequent devastation of the Bandanese people in seventeenth-century Indonesia. Ghosh further complicates the narrative. The Banda Islands are shown to have been sitting upon 'one of the fault lines where the Earth shows itself to be most palpably alive'. This refers to how the islands and their volcanoes are an integral part of the Circum-Pacific Belt which is a path along the Pacific Ocean characterized by active volcanoes and frequent earthquakes.\\n\\nGhosh remains finely sensitive to botany, when he talks about the 'trees and their offspring', adding how they 'were of very different temperaments'. 'The trees were home-loving and did not venture out of their native Maluku until the eighteenth century. Nutmegs and mace, on the other hand, were tireless travelers'. This kind of description almost reworks some form of animism in which 'non-humans' are delineated with agency.\\n\\nGhosh also animates details. Consider how he describes the nutmeg: 'Like a planet, the nutmeg is encased within a series of expanding spheres'. He then proceeds to peel off the layers of the nutmeg to light up an analogy with Earth's atmosphere. The author underlines that 'Humanity is being so closely entangled with the products of the Earth that the past cannot be remembered without them'. He calls for us to reevaluate the belief that 'resources or commodities', as we usually know of things such as the nutmeg, 'have no world- or history-making powers of their own'.\\n\\nYet, the author remains alert to the predicaments of the geopolitical order, which come out in stark detail when he talks about 'non-renewables' as 'resources'. When he puts James Lovelock's Gaia hypothesis to work, his dilemmas become evident. The first half of the book arguably emerges as an attempt to supplant simplified histories of colonialism with the complexities of geology and botany; conversely, the writing of 'commodities' as having their 'own histories' in the second half ultimately seems to fall back on the same distinction made by R.G. Collingwood. Collingwood differentiated between human history and natural history by remarking that 'all history properly so called is the history of human affairs'.\\n\\nBy writing a history of colonialism, he falls back on the choices of the present that repetitively ask the same question: who is responsible for climate change? Of course, it is human-induced and anthropogenic, as even the policy documents testify. But on what scale and through what measures? Even while using the concept of Gaia frequently, Ghosh fails to show how the living organisms on the planet interact with their surrounding inorganic environment to configure a synergetic and self-regulating system that originally created the climate and biochemical conditions sufficient and responsible for creating 'life' on this planet. This is because Ghosh still holds humans closest to his heart. A book that makes the case for a 'planet in crisis' intentionally emphasizes human parables alone.","question":"It can be inferred from the passage that Ghosh uses all of the following in his narration of colonial histories in The Nutmeg's Curse EXCEPT","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"geology","B":"the life sciences","C":"planetarity","D":"artistic echoes"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"artistic echoes","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","C":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — geology is explicitly cited: 'the complexities of geology and botany' and the Circum-Pacific Belt description. Choice B is incorrect — botany and the life sciences are explicitly referenced in Ghosh's treatment of trees, nutmegs and mace. Choice C is incorrect — planetarity is present in the nutmeg-as-planet analogy: 'Like a planet, the nutmeg is encased within a series of expanding spheres'. Choice D is CORRECT — no artistic echoes (visual art, music, literary allusion in an artistic sense) are evident in the passage's account of Ghosh's methods; the passage describes geology, botany, animism, and planetary analogy, but not artistic reference.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E7_002","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P1","passage":"In The Nutmeg's Curse: Parables for a Planet in Crisis, Amitav Ghosh explores the spice nutmeg as a parable for our contemporary climate crisis, showing how human history has always been entangled with earthly materials. Offering a lucid call to action that seeks to inspire a collective response to the crisis, this book is urgent reading. In The Nutmeg's Curse, Ghosh recounts the histories of Western colonialism through the nutmeg. Yes, the spice that was originally found in the Banda Islands of Indonesia and has since travelled far and wide at the hands of trade and commerce to be present in kitchens across the world. What comes out of this fairly familiar trope of exploring a global commodity like nutmeg is a critical history of colonialism and capitalism that has been frequently told.\\n\\nIn the first chapter titled 'A Lamp Falls', we are not only introduced to the Dutch East India Company, islanders' resistance to it and the subsequent devastation of the Bandanese people in seventeenth-century Indonesia. Ghosh further complicates the narrative. The Banda Islands are shown to have been sitting upon 'one of the fault lines where the Earth shows itself to be most palpably alive'. This refers to how the islands and their volcanoes are an integral part of the Circum-Pacific Belt which is a path along the Pacific Ocean characterized by active volcanoes and frequent earthquakes.\\n\\nGhosh remains finely sensitive to botany, when he talks about the 'trees and their offspring', adding how they 'were of very different temperaments'. 'The trees were home-loving and did not venture out of their native Maluku until the eighteenth century. Nutmegs and mace, on the other hand, were tireless travelers'. This kind of description almost reworks some form of animism in which 'non-humans' are delineated with agency.\\n\\nGhosh also animates details. Consider how he describes the nutmeg: 'Like a planet, the nutmeg is encased within a series of expanding spheres'. He then proceeds to peel off the layers of the nutmeg to light up an analogy with Earth's atmosphere. The author underlines that 'Humanity is being so closely entangled with the products of the Earth that the past cannot be remembered without them'. He calls for us to reevaluate the belief that 'resources or commodities', as we usually know of things such as the nutmeg, 'have no world- or history-making powers of their own'.\\n\\nYet, the author remains alert to the predicaments of the geopolitical order, which come out in stark detail when he talks about 'non-renewables' as 'resources'. When he puts James Lovelock's Gaia hypothesis to work, his dilemmas become evident. The first half of the book arguably emerges as an attempt to supplant simplified histories of colonialism with the complexities of geology and botany; conversely, the writing of 'commodities' as having their 'own histories' in the second half ultimately seems to fall back on the same distinction made by R.G. Collingwood. Collingwood differentiated between human history and natural history by remarking that 'all history properly so called is the history of human affairs'.\\n\\nBy writing a history of colonialism, he falls back on the choices of the present that repetitively ask the same question: who is responsible for climate change? Of course, it is human-induced and anthropogenic, as even the policy documents testify. But on what scale and through what measures? Even while using the concept of Gaia frequently, Ghosh fails to show how the living organisms on the planet interact with their surrounding inorganic environment to configure a synergetic and self-regulating system that originally created the climate and biochemical conditions sufficient and responsible for creating 'life' on this planet. This is because Ghosh still holds humans closest to his heart. A book that makes the case for a 'planet in crisis' intentionally emphasizes human parables alone.","question":"It can be inferred from the passage that the agenda of The Nutmeg's Curse is to","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"examine the consequences of climate change on people.","B":"identify colonialism as fanning the flames of the climate crisis.","C":"make a case for colonialism leading to climate change through resource exploitation.","D":"recognize climate change as an unremitting problem that is aggravating communities' exposure to it."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"make a case for colonialism leading to climate change through resource exploitation.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"correct_but_unstated","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the book's agenda is not to examine climate change's consequences on people in isolation; it links colonialism, resource exploitation, and the climate crisis through the nutmeg parable; this is too narrow. Choice B is incorrect — while colonialism and climate change are linked in the book, the passage's specific framing is that nutmeg serves as a parable showing how colonial resource exploitation drives the climate crisis; 'fanning the flames' is imprecise and understates the causal argument. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage states Ghosh 'explores the spice nutmeg as a parable for our contemporary climate crisis' and that 'exploring a global commodity like nutmeg is a critical history of colonialism and capitalism', establishing the book's case that colonial resource exploitation is the thread connecting past and present crisis. Choice D is incorrect — aggravating community exposure to climate change is not the book's agenda as described; this introduces a framing not in the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E7_003","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P1","passage":"In The Nutmeg's Curse: Parables for a Planet in Crisis, Amitav Ghosh explores the spice nutmeg as a parable for our contemporary climate crisis, showing how human history has always been entangled with earthly materials. Offering a lucid call to action that seeks to inspire a collective response to the crisis, this book is urgent reading. In The Nutmeg's Curse, Ghosh recounts the histories of Western colonialism through the nutmeg. Yes, the spice that was originally found in the Banda Islands of Indonesia and has since travelled far and wide at the hands of trade and commerce to be present in kitchens across the world. What comes out of this fairly familiar trope of exploring a global commodity like nutmeg is a critical history of colonialism and capitalism that has been frequently told.\\n\\nIn the first chapter titled 'A Lamp Falls', we are not only introduced to the Dutch East India Company, islanders' resistance to it and the subsequent devastation of the Bandanese people in seventeenth-century Indonesia. Ghosh further complicates the narrative. The Banda Islands are shown to have been sitting upon 'one of the fault lines where the Earth shows itself to be most palpably alive'. This refers to how the islands and their volcanoes are an integral part of the Circum-Pacific Belt which is a path along the Pacific Ocean characterized by active volcanoes and frequent earthquakes.\\n\\nGhosh remains finely sensitive to botany, when he talks about the 'trees and their offspring', adding how they 'were of very different temperaments'. 'The trees were home-loving and did not venture out of their native Maluku until the eighteenth century. Nutmegs and mace, on the other hand, were tireless travelers'. This kind of description almost reworks some form of animism in which 'non-humans' are delineated with agency.\\n\\nGhosh also animates details. Consider how he describes the nutmeg: 'Like a planet, the nutmeg is encased within a series of expanding spheres'. He then proceeds to peel off the layers of the nutmeg to light up an analogy with Earth's atmosphere. The author underlines that 'Humanity is being so closely entangled with the products of the Earth that the past cannot be remembered without them'. He calls for us to reevaluate the belief that 'resources or commodities', as we usually know of things such as the nutmeg, 'have no world- or history-making powers of their own'.\\n\\nYet, the author remains alert to the predicaments of the geopolitical order, which come out in stark detail when he talks about 'non-renewables' as 'resources'. When he puts James Lovelock's Gaia hypothesis to work, his dilemmas become evident. The first half of the book arguably emerges as an attempt to supplant simplified histories of colonialism with the complexities of geology and botany; conversely, the writing of 'commodities' as having their 'own histories' in the second half ultimately seems to fall back on the same distinction made by R.G. Collingwood. Collingwood differentiated between human history and natural history by remarking that 'all history properly so called is the history of human affairs'.\\n\\nBy writing a history of colonialism, he falls back on the choices of the present that repetitively ask the same question: who is responsible for climate change? Of course, it is human-induced and anthropogenic, as even the policy documents testify. But on what scale and through what measures? Even while using the concept of Gaia frequently, Ghosh fails to show how the living organisms on the planet interact with their surrounding inorganic environment to configure a synergetic and self-regulating system that originally created the climate and biochemical conditions sufficient and responsible for creating 'life' on this planet. This is because Ghosh still holds humans closest to his heart. A book that makes the case for a 'planet in crisis' intentionally emphasizes human parables alone.","question":"The author is likely to agree with all of the following statements EXCEPT","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The indigenous Bandanese communities paid a high price for access to nutmegs.","B":"The devastating fate of the Banda Islands forewarns of a threat to our present day.","C":"A colonial mindset does not justify the exploitation of the natural environment.","D":"Human history is shaped by non-human forces."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"A colonial mindset does not justify the exploitation of the natural environment.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the reviewer supports this; the passage explicitly references 'the subsequent devastation of the Bandanese people', which the reviewer treats as a stated fact. Choice B is incorrect — the reviewer supports this; the book is titled 'Parables for a Planet in Crisis' and the reviewer endorses its urgent framing. Choice C is CORRECT — the reviewer would not agree with this statement as written because, per the passage, Ghosh argues the colonial mindset did justify such exploitation — that justification is precisely what the book critiques; saying it 'does not justify' misrepresents the book's argument that it did and still does. Choice D is incorrect — the reviewer supports this; the passage endorses Ghosh's call to reevaluate the belief that commodities 'have no world- or history-making powers of their own'.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E7_004","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P1","passage":"In The Nutmeg's Curse: Parables for a Planet in Crisis, Amitav Ghosh explores the spice nutmeg as a parable for our contemporary climate crisis, showing how human history has always been entangled with earthly materials. Offering a lucid call to action that seeks to inspire a collective response to the crisis, this book is urgent reading. In The Nutmeg's Curse, Ghosh recounts the histories of Western colonialism through the nutmeg. Yes, the spice that was originally found in the Banda Islands of Indonesia and has since travelled far and wide at the hands of trade and commerce to be present in kitchens across the world. What comes out of this fairly familiar trope of exploring a global commodity like nutmeg is a critical history of colonialism and capitalism that has been frequently told.\\n\\nIn the first chapter titled 'A Lamp Falls', we are not only introduced to the Dutch East India Company, islanders' resistance to it and the subsequent devastation of the Bandanese people in seventeenth-century Indonesia. Ghosh further complicates the narrative. The Banda Islands are shown to have been sitting upon 'one of the fault lines where the Earth shows itself to be most palpably alive'. This refers to how the islands and their volcanoes are an integral part of the Circum-Pacific Belt which is a path along the Pacific Ocean characterized by active volcanoes and frequent earthquakes.\\n\\nGhosh remains finely sensitive to botany, when he talks about the 'trees and their offspring', adding how they 'were of very different temperaments'. 'The trees were home-loving and did not venture out of their native Maluku until the eighteenth century. Nutmegs and mace, on the other hand, were tireless travelers'. This kind of description almost reworks some form of animism in which 'non-humans' are delineated with agency.\\n\\nGhosh also animates details. Consider how he describes the nutmeg: 'Like a planet, the nutmeg is encased within a series of expanding spheres'. He then proceeds to peel off the layers of the nutmeg to light up an analogy with Earth's atmosphere. The author underlines that 'Humanity is being so closely entangled with the products of the Earth that the past cannot be remembered without them'. He calls for us to reevaluate the belief that 'resources or commodities', as we usually know of things such as the nutmeg, 'have no world- or history-making powers of their own'.\\n\\nYet, the author remains alert to the predicaments of the geopolitical order, which come out in stark detail when he talks about 'non-renewables' as 'resources'. When he puts James Lovelock's Gaia hypothesis to work, his dilemmas become evident. The first half of the book arguably emerges as an attempt to supplant simplified histories of colonialism with the complexities of geology and botany; conversely, the writing of 'commodities' as having their 'own histories' in the second half ultimately seems to fall back on the same distinction made by R.G. Collingwood. Collingwood differentiated between human history and natural history by remarking that 'all history properly so called is the history of human affairs'.\\n\\nBy writing a history of colonialism, he falls back on the choices of the present that repetitively ask the same question: who is responsible for climate change? Of course, it is human-induced and anthropogenic, as even the policy documents testify. But on what scale and through what measures? Even while using the concept of Gaia frequently, Ghosh fails to show how the living organisms on the planet interact with their surrounding inorganic environment to configure a synergetic and self-regulating system that originally created the climate and biochemical conditions sufficient and responsible for creating 'life' on this planet. This is because Ghosh still holds humans closest to his heart. A book that makes the case for a 'planet in crisis' intentionally emphasizes human parables alone.","question":"The passage identifies which of the following as a shortcoming in Amitav Ghosh's The Nutmeg's Curse?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Exploring the impact of the story about nutmegs in topical time and space","B":"The evocation of agency to nutmegs and mace as temperamental travelers who pass continental hands","C":"Coming to a consensus on the reasons for human-induced climate change but not ensuring that the message circulates","D":"Going with the conventional distinction between natural history and human affairs"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Going with the conventional distinction between natural history and human affairs","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"contradicts_passage","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — exploring the nutmeg's story in time and space is not identified as a shortcoming in the passage; it is part of Ghosh's method. Choice B is incorrect — attributing agency to nutmegs and mace is presented positively as animism and as a strength of the book, not a shortcoming; this contradicts the passage's tone. Choice C is incorrect — message circulation is not mentioned as a shortcoming in the passage; this introduces a concern not raised by the reviewer. Choice D is CORRECT — the passage explicitly states that the second half of the book 'ultimately seems to fall back on the same distinction made by R.G. Collingwood', who separated human history from natural history; reverting to this conventional distinction is identified as the book's central shortcoming.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E7_005","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P1","passage":"In The Nutmeg's Curse: Parables for a Planet in Crisis, Amitav Ghosh explores the spice nutmeg as a parable for our contemporary climate crisis, showing how human history has always been entangled with earthly materials. Offering a lucid call to action that seeks to inspire a collective response to the crisis, this book is urgent reading. In The Nutmeg's Curse, Ghosh recounts the histories of Western colonialism through the nutmeg. Yes, the spice that was originally found in the Banda Islands of Indonesia and has since travelled far and wide at the hands of trade and commerce to be present in kitchens across the world. What comes out of this fairly familiar trope of exploring a global commodity like nutmeg is a critical history of colonialism and capitalism that has been frequently told.\\n\\nIn the first chapter titled 'A Lamp Falls', we are not only introduced to the Dutch East India Company, islanders' resistance to it and the subsequent devastation of the Bandanese people in seventeenth-century Indonesia. Ghosh further complicates the narrative. The Banda Islands are shown to have been sitting upon 'one of the fault lines where the Earth shows itself to be most palpably alive'. This refers to how the islands and their volcanoes are an integral part of the Circum-Pacific Belt which is a path along the Pacific Ocean characterized by active volcanoes and frequent earthquakes.\\n\\nGhosh remains finely sensitive to botany, when he talks about the 'trees and their offspring', adding how they 'were of very different temperaments'. 'The trees were home-loving and did not venture out of their native Maluku until the eighteenth century. Nutmegs and mace, on the other hand, were tireless travelers'. This kind of description almost reworks some form of animism in which 'non-humans' are delineated with agency.\\n\\nGhosh also animates details. Consider how he describes the nutmeg: 'Like a planet, the nutmeg is encased within a series of expanding spheres'. He then proceeds to peel off the layers of the nutmeg to light up an analogy with Earth's atmosphere. The author underlines that 'Humanity is being so closely entangled with the products of the Earth that the past cannot be remembered without them'. He calls for us to reevaluate the belief that 'resources or commodities', as we usually know of things such as the nutmeg, 'have no world- or history-making powers of their own'.\\n\\nYet, the author remains alert to the predicaments of the geopolitical order, which come out in stark detail when he talks about 'non-renewables' as 'resources'. When he puts James Lovelock's Gaia hypothesis to work, his dilemmas become evident. The first half of the book arguably emerges as an attempt to supplant simplified histories of colonialism with the complexities of geology and botany; conversely, the writing of 'commodities' as having their 'own histories' in the second half ultimately seems to fall back on the same distinction made by R.G. Collingwood. Collingwood differentiated between human history and natural history by remarking that 'all history properly so called is the history of human affairs'.\\n\\nBy writing a history of colonialism, he falls back on the choices of the present that repetitively ask the same question: who is responsible for climate change? Of course, it is human-induced and anthropogenic, as even the policy documents testify. But on what scale and through what measures? Even while using the concept of Gaia frequently, Ghosh fails to show how the living organisms on the planet interact with their surrounding inorganic environment to configure a synergetic and self-regulating system that originally created the climate and biochemical conditions sufficient and responsible for creating 'life' on this planet. This is because Ghosh still holds humans closest to his heart. A book that makes the case for a 'planet in crisis' intentionally emphasizes human parables alone.","question":"The passage characterizes James Lovelock's Gaia hypothesis as a ______________ system on the Earth.","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"disruptive and chaotic","B":"holistic and interacting","C":"regulating and diminishing","D":"catalytic and expedient"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"holistic and interacting","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","C":"correct_but_unstated","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the Gaia hypothesis as described in the passage is self-regulating and synergetic, not disruptive and chaotic; this contradicts the passage. Choice B is CORRECT — the passage describes Gaia as 'a synergetic and self-regulating system' in which 'living organisms on the planet interact with their surrounding inorganic environment', making it holistic (whole-system) and interacting (organisms interacting with their environment). Choice C is incorrect — 'self-regulating' is in the passage but 'diminishing' is not; combining a correct element with a wrong one makes this option inaccurate. Choice D is incorrect — neither 'catalytic' nor 'expedient' (the latter implying questionable motive) is used to characterise Gaia in the passage; these terms are out of scope.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E7_006","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P2","passage":"There is a basic discipline that makes teams work. Teams and good performance are inseparable. But people use the word team so loosely that it gets in the way of learning and applying the discipline that leads to good performance. For managers to make better decisions about whether, when, or how to encourage and use teams, it is important to be more precise about what a team is and what it isn't.\\n\\nTeamwork represents a set of values that encourage listening and responding constructively to views expressed by others, giving others the benefit of the doubt, providing support, and recognizing the interests and achievements of others. Such values help teams perform, and they also promote individual performance and the performance of an entire organization. But teamwork values by themselves are not exclusive to teams, nor are they enough to ensure team performance.\\n\\nNor is a team just any group working together. Committees, councils, and task forces are not necessarily teams. The entire work force of any large and complex organization is never a team, but think about how often that platitude is offered up.\\n\\nTo understand how teams deliver extra performance, we must distinguish between teams and other forms of working groups. That distinction turns on performance results. A working group's performance is a function of what its members do as individuals. A team's performance includes both individual results and what we call \\"collective work-products\\". A collective work-product is what two or more members must work on together, such as interviews, surveys, or experiments. A collective work-product reflects the joint, real contribution of team members.\\n\\nWorking groups are both prevalent and effective in large organizations where individual accountability is most important. The best working groups come together to share information, perspectives, and insights, to make decisions that help each person do his or her job better; and to reinforce individual performance standards. But the focus is always on individual goals and accountabilities. Working-group members don't take responsibility for results other than their own.\\n\\nTeams differ fundamentally from working groups because they require both individual and mutual accountability. Teams rely on more than group's discussion, debate, and decision; on more than sharing information and best practice performance standards. Teams produce discrete work-products through the joint contributions of their members. This is what makes possible performance levels greater than the sum of all the individual bests of team members. Simply stated, a team is more than the sum of its parts.\\n\\nThe essence of a team is common commitment. Without it, groups perform as individuals; with it, they become a powerful unit of collective performance. This kind of commitment requires a purpose in which team members can believe. Credible team purposes have an element related to winning, being first, revolutionizing, or being on the cutting edge.","question":"According to the author,","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"team and good performance are two different things.","B":"discipline makes the difference between a team that performs and one that does not.","C":"team values always help raise the level of performance of a group.","D":"None of the above"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"discipline makes the difference between a team that performs and one that does not.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","C":"too_extreme","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage opens with 'Teams and good performance are inseparable', directly contradicting the idea that they are two different things. Choice B is CORRECT — the passage opens 'There is a basic discipline that makes teams work', identifying discipline as the key differentiator for team performance. Choice C is incorrect — the passage states teamwork values 'are not exclusive to teams, nor are they enough to ensure team performance'; saying they 'always' raise group performance overstates what the passage claims. Choice D is incorrect — Choice B is clearly supported by the first sentence of the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E7_007","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P2","passage":"There is a basic discipline that makes teams work. Teams and good performance are inseparable. But people use the word team so loosely that it gets in the way of learning and applying the discipline that leads to good performance. For managers to make better decisions about whether, when, or how to encourage and use teams, it is important to be more precise about what a team is and what it isn't.\\n\\nTeamwork represents a set of values that encourage listening and responding constructively to views expressed by others, giving others the benefit of the doubt, providing support, and recognizing the interests and achievements of others. Such values help teams perform, and they also promote individual performance and the performance of an entire organization. But teamwork values by themselves are not exclusive to teams, nor are they enough to ensure team performance.\\n\\nNor is a team just any group working together. Committees, councils, and task forces are not necessarily teams. The entire work force of any large and complex organization is never a team, but think about how often that platitude is offered up.\\n\\nTo understand how teams deliver extra performance, we must distinguish between teams and other forms of working groups. That distinction turns on performance results. A working group's performance is a function of what its members do as individuals. A team's performance includes both individual results and what we call \\"collective work-products\\". A collective work-product is what two or more members must work on together, such as interviews, surveys, or experiments. A collective work-product reflects the joint, real contribution of team members.\\n\\nWorking groups are both prevalent and effective in large organizations where individual accountability is most important. The best working groups come together to share information, perspectives, and insights, to make decisions that help each person do his or her job better; and to reinforce individual performance standards. But the focus is always on individual goals and accountabilities. Working-group members don't take responsibility for results other than their own.\\n\\nTeams differ fundamentally from working groups because they require both individual and mutual accountability. Teams rely on more than group's discussion, debate, and decision; on more than sharing information and best practice performance standards. Teams produce discrete work-products through the joint contributions of their members. This is what makes possible performance levels greater than the sum of all the individual bests of team members. Simply stated, a team is more than the sum of its parts.\\n\\nThe essence of a team is common commitment. Without it, groups perform as individuals; with it, they become a powerful unit of collective performance. This kind of commitment requires a purpose in which team members can believe. Credible team purposes have an element related to winning, being first, revolutionizing, or being on the cutting edge.","question":"The author is of the opinion that no group becomes a team unless there is","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"mutual accountability.","B":"mutual commitment.","C":"mutual goals.","D":"All of the above"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"All of the above","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"correct_but_unstated","C":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — mutual accountability is stated in paragraph 6 as necessary, but picking this alone misses the other requirements. Choice B is incorrect — common commitment (mutual commitment) is stated in paragraph 7 as the 'essence of a team', but picking this alone misses the others. Choice C is incorrect — mutual goals are implied by 'a purpose in which team members can believe' in paragraph 7, but picking this alone misses the others. Choice D is CORRECT — the passage identifies all three: mutual accountability (paragraph 6), common commitment/purpose (paragraph 7), and mutual goals (paragraph 7); all three together make a group a team. Students who pick A, B or C stop checking after finding one valid element.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E7_008","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P2","passage":"There is a basic discipline that makes teams work. Teams and good performance are inseparable. But people use the word team so loosely that it gets in the way of learning and applying the discipline that leads to good performance. For managers to make better decisions about whether, when, or how to encourage and use teams, it is important to be more precise about what a team is and what it isn't.\\n\\nTeamwork represents a set of values that encourage listening and responding constructively to views expressed by others, giving others the benefit of the doubt, providing support, and recognizing the interests and achievements of others. Such values help teams perform, and they also promote individual performance and the performance of an entire organization. But teamwork values by themselves are not exclusive to teams, nor are they enough to ensure team performance.\\n\\nNor is a team just any group working together. Committees, councils, and task forces are not necessarily teams. The entire work force of any large and complex organization is never a team, but think about how often that platitude is offered up.\\n\\nTo understand how teams deliver extra performance, we must distinguish between teams and other forms of working groups. That distinction turns on performance results. A working group's performance is a function of what its members do as individuals. A team's performance includes both individual results and what we call \\"collective work-products\\". A collective work-product is what two or more members must work on together, such as interviews, surveys, or experiments. A collective work-product reflects the joint, real contribution of team members.\\n\\nWorking groups are both prevalent and effective in large organizations where individual accountability is most important. The best working groups come together to share information, perspectives, and insights, to make decisions that help each person do his or her job better; and to reinforce individual performance standards. But the focus is always on individual goals and accountabilities. Working-group members don't take responsibility for results other than their own.\\n\\nTeams differ fundamentally from working groups because they require both individual and mutual accountability. Teams rely on more than group's discussion, debate, and decision; on more than sharing information and best practice performance standards. Teams produce discrete work-products through the joint contributions of their members. This is what makes possible performance levels greater than the sum of all the individual bests of team members. Simply stated, a team is more than the sum of its parts.\\n\\nThe essence of a team is common commitment. Without it, groups perform as individuals; with it, they become a powerful unit of collective performance. This kind of commitment requires a purpose in which team members can believe. Credible team purposes have an element related to winning, being first, revolutionizing, or being on the cutting edge.","question":"Team values include all the following factors except","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"supporting.","B":"encouraging.","C":"delegating.","D":"recognizing each person's contribution."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"delegating.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — 'providing support' is explicitly listed in paragraph 2 as a team value. Choice B is incorrect — while the exact word 'encouraging' is not used, 'giving others the benefit of the doubt' and 'responding constructively' are closely related values listed in the passage; this is close enough to be treated as stated. Choice C is CORRECT — delegating is not mentioned anywhere in the passage as a team value; it is a management function, not a teamwork value as defined by the passage. Choice D is incorrect — 'recognizing the interests and achievements of others' is explicitly listed in paragraph 2 as a team value.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E7_009","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P2","passage":"There is a basic discipline that makes teams work. Teams and good performance are inseparable. But people use the word team so loosely that it gets in the way of learning and applying the discipline that leads to good performance. For managers to make better decisions about whether, when, or how to encourage and use teams, it is important to be more precise about what a team is and what it isn't.\\n\\nTeamwork represents a set of values that encourage listening and responding constructively to views expressed by others, giving others the benefit of the doubt, providing support, and recognizing the interests and achievements of others. Such values help teams perform, and they also promote individual performance and the performance of an entire organization. But teamwork values by themselves are not exclusive to teams, nor are they enough to ensure team performance.\\n\\nNor is a team just any group working together. Committees, councils, and task forces are not necessarily teams. The entire work force of any large and complex organization is never a team, but think about how often that platitude is offered up.\\n\\nTo understand how teams deliver extra performance, we must distinguish between teams and other forms of working groups. That distinction turns on performance results. A working group's performance is a function of what its members do as individuals. A team's performance includes both individual results and what we call \\"collective work-products\\". A collective work-product is what two or more members must work on together, such as interviews, surveys, or experiments. A collective work-product reflects the joint, real contribution of team members.\\n\\nWorking groups are both prevalent and effective in large organizations where individual accountability is most important. The best working groups come together to share information, perspectives, and insights, to make decisions that help each person do his or her job better; and to reinforce individual performance standards. But the focus is always on individual goals and accountabilities. Working-group members don't take responsibility for results other than their own.\\n\\nTeams differ fundamentally from working groups because they require both individual and mutual accountability. Teams rely on more than group's discussion, debate, and decision; on more than sharing information and best practice performance standards. Teams produce discrete work-products through the joint contributions of their members. This is what makes possible performance levels greater than the sum of all the individual bests of team members. Simply stated, a team is more than the sum of its parts.\\n\\nThe essence of a team is common commitment. Without it, groups perform as individuals; with it, they become a powerful unit of collective performance. This kind of commitment requires a purpose in which team members can believe. Credible team purposes have an element related to winning, being first, revolutionizing, or being on the cutting edge.","question":"The above passage is most likely taken from","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"a book on team building.","B":"a newspaper article on group dynamics.","C":"an article from a management journal.","D":"None of the above"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"a book on team building.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"irrelevant_comparison","C":"correct_but_unstated","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — the passage is structured, formal, and educational in tone with a sustained focus on defining teams, differentiating them from groups, and establishing what makes teams perform; this is characteristic of a book-length treatment of team building, not a short-form article. Choice B is incorrect — a newspaper article would be shorter and more journalistic; this passage is analytical and definitional, more consistent with a book; 'group dynamics' in the option description also overstates what the passage covers. Choice C is incorrect — while a management journal article is plausible, the sustained pedagogical structure (definitions, distinctions, numbered concepts) points more to a book chapter than a journal article. Choice D is incorrect — Choice A is clearly supported.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E7_010","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P2","passage":"There is a basic discipline that makes teams work. Teams and good performance are inseparable. But people use the word team so loosely that it gets in the way of learning and applying the discipline that leads to good performance. For managers to make better decisions about whether, when, or how to encourage and use teams, it is important to be more precise about what a team is and what it isn't.\\n\\nTeamwork represents a set of values that encourage listening and responding constructively to views expressed by others, giving others the benefit of the doubt, providing support, and recognizing the interests and achievements of others. Such values help teams perform, and they also promote individual performance and the performance of an entire organization. But teamwork values by themselves are not exclusive to teams, nor are they enough to ensure team performance.\\n\\nNor is a team just any group working together. Committees, councils, and task forces are not necessarily teams. The entire work force of any large and complex organization is never a team, but think about how often that platitude is offered up.\\n\\nTo understand how teams deliver extra performance, we must distinguish between teams and other forms of working groups. That distinction turns on performance results. A working group's performance is a function of what its members do as individuals. A team's performance includes both individual results and what we call \\"collective work-products\\". A collective work-product is what two or more members must work on together, such as interviews, surveys, or experiments. A collective work-product reflects the joint, real contribution of team members.\\n\\nWorking groups are both prevalent and effective in large organizations where individual accountability is most important. The best working groups come together to share information, perspectives, and insights, to make decisions that help each person do his or her job better; and to reinforce individual performance standards. But the focus is always on individual goals and accountabilities. Working-group members don't take responsibility for results other than their own.\\n\\nTeams differ fundamentally from working groups because they require both individual and mutual accountability. Teams rely on more than group's discussion, debate, and decision; on more than sharing information and best practice performance standards. Teams produce discrete work-products through the joint contributions of their members. This is what makes possible performance levels greater than the sum of all the individual bests of team members. Simply stated, a team is more than the sum of its parts.\\n\\nThe essence of a team is common commitment. Without it, groups perform as individuals; with it, they become a powerful unit of collective performance. This kind of commitment requires a purpose in which team members can believe. Credible team purposes have an element related to winning, being first, revolutionizing, or being on the cutting edge.","question":"We can infer from the passage that synergy is a characteristic of","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"work groups.","B":"high performing teams.","C":"committees.","D":"All the above"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"high performing teams.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"too_extreme"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage explicitly states that working groups focus on individual accountability and individual results; synergy (output greater than the sum of parts) is specifically attributed to teams, not working groups; this contradicts the passage. Choice B is CORRECT — the passage states teams produce 'performance levels greater than the sum of all the individual bests of team members' — this is the definition of synergy; it is a characteristic of high-performing teams. Choice C is incorrect — the passage states 'committees, councils, and task forces are not necessarily teams'; they are working groups, and synergy is not attributed to them. Choice D is incorrect — since A and C are wrong, 'all of the above' is incorrect; students selecting this fail to distinguish teams from working groups.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E7_011","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P2","passage":"There is a basic discipline that makes teams work. Teams and good performance are inseparable. But people use the word team so loosely that it gets in the way of learning and applying the discipline that leads to good performance. For managers to make better decisions about whether, when, or how to encourage and use teams, it is important to be more precise about what a team is and what it isn't.\\n\\nTeamwork represents a set of values that encourage listening and responding constructively to views expressed by others, giving others the benefit of the doubt, providing support, and recognizing the interests and achievements of others. Such values help teams perform, and they also promote individual performance and the performance of an entire organization. But teamwork values by themselves are not exclusive to teams, nor are they enough to ensure team performance.\\n\\nNor is a team just any group working together. Committees, councils, and task forces are not necessarily teams. The entire work force of any large and complex organization is never a team, but think about how often that platitude is offered up.\\n\\nTo understand how teams deliver extra performance, we must distinguish between teams and other forms of working groups. That distinction turns on performance results. A working group's performance is a function of what its members do as individuals. A team's performance includes both individual results and what we call \\"collective work-products\\". A collective work-product is what two or more members must work on together, such as interviews, surveys, or experiments. A collective work-product reflects the joint, real contribution of team members.\\n\\nWorking groups are both prevalent and effective in large organizations where individual accountability is most important. The best working groups come together to share information, perspectives, and insights, to make decisions that help each person do his or her job better; and to reinforce individual performance standards. But the focus is always on individual goals and accountabilities. Working-group members don't take responsibility for results other than their own.\\n\\nTeams differ fundamentally from working groups because they require both individual and mutual accountability. Teams rely on more than group's discussion, debate, and decision; on more than sharing information and best practice performance standards. Teams produce discrete work-products through the joint contributions of their members. This is what makes possible performance levels greater than the sum of all the individual bests of team members. Simply stated, a team is more than the sum of its parts.\\n\\nThe essence of a team is common commitment. Without it, groups perform as individuals; with it, they become a powerful unit of collective performance. This kind of commitment requires a purpose in which team members can believe. Credible team purposes have an element related to winning, being first, revolutionizing, or being on the cutting edge.","question":"The author's tone in the passage can be best described as","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"analytical.","B":"opinionated.","C":"dogmatic.","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"analytical.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — the author defines terms, draws distinctions between teams and working groups, and systematically examines characteristics of each; this is a textbook analytical approach. Choice B is incorrect — the author does not present strong personal opinions or argue passionately for a position; the tone is definitional and classificatory, not opinionated. Choice C is incorrect — dogmatic implies asserting unproved principles as absolute truth; the author is careful to define and distinguish, not to assert doctrine. Choice D is incorrect — Choice A is clearly the right descriptor.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E7_012","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P3","passage":"The invention of the means of being comfortable and the pursuit of comfort as a desirable end are modern phenomena, unparalleled in history since the time of the Romans. The padded-chain, the well spring bed, the sofa, central heating, and the regular hot bath and a host of other comforts enter into the daily lives of even the most moderately prosperous of the Anglo-Saxon bourgeoisie. Three hundred years ago, these were unknown to the greatest king.\\n\\nThe first thing that strikes one about the discomfort in which our ancestors lived is that it was mainly voluntary. Some of the apparatus of modern comfort is of purely modern invention; people could not put rubber tyres on their carriage before the discovery of rubber plant. But for the most part, there is nothing new about the material basis of our comfort. Men could have installed bathrooms and central heating and sanitary plumbing any time during the last three or four thousand years. Two thousand years before Christ, the inhabitants of Cnossos were familiar with sanitary plumbing. The Romans had invented an elaborate system of hot air heating, and the bathing facilities in a smart Roman villa were luxurious and completely beyond the dreams of modern man.\\n\\nIf the men of Middle Ages and early modern epoch lived in dirt and discomfort, it was not for any lack or ability to change their mode of life, it was because they chose to live in this way, because it fitted in with their principles and prejudices - political, moral, religious. What have comfort and cleanliness to do with politics, morals and religions? Let us begin with the consideration of armchair and heating.\\n\\nArmchairs and central heating became possible only with the breakdown of the power of kings, and great lords and the decay of social classes and the old family system. In the past, human society was a hierarchy of ranks in which every man was always engaged in being impressive towards his inferiors or respectful to those above him. Relaxing in armchair in such societies was utterly impossible. Old furniture reflects the physical habits of the class or society for which it was made.\\n\\nAnother essential component of modern comfort – the proper heating of houses – was made impossible for the great ones of the earth by the political structure of ancient societies. The great had to live in great palaces, with large and lofty halls in which they dined in state or received their guests. Splendid, but cold and drafty. Today, the self-made great men have not to keep up their positions in the splendid style of those who were born great, sacrificing grandeur to comfort, they live in rooms small enough to be heated. The third great comfort of modern age, the bath, is due, partly to the weakening of the tradition of religious asceticism which did not set much store by cleanliness. But it is principally to the doctors that bath-lovers owe their greatest debt. The discovery of infection through germs has put a premium on cleanliness. We wash now with religious fervour. Our baths have become something like magic rites to protect us from the powers of evil, embodied in the dirt-loving germ. Since the discovery of the beneficial effects of sunlight, too much clothing has become, medically speaking, a sin. And now comfort has not only become a physical habit but a fashion. But the achievement of comfort has been accompanied by a loss of other equally perhaps more valuable things. A man of means who builds a house today is in general concerned primarily with the comfort of his future residence. His ancient counterpart would have been primarily concerned with the impressiveness and magnificence of his dwelling – with beauty rather than comfort. Is our present passion for comfort a little exaggerated? Should not comfort be rightly regarded as a means to an end, as intended to create conditions which are favourable to the life of the mankind?","question":"What, according to the passage, was the condition of human society in the past?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Only the aristocrats and bureaucrats had access to methods of making life comfortable.","B":"Using armchairs, central heating systems, sanitary plumbing etc., was not considered respectable.","C":"There was a hierarchy in human society where people tried to impress both their inferiors and superiors.","D":"Using old furniture was one of the signs of aristocracy."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"There was a hierarchy in human society where people tried to impress both their inferiors and superiors.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage does not say only aristocrats had access to comfort methods; it says medieval people chose not to use them for principled reasons; access was not the limiting factor. Choice B is incorrect — the passage does not say these comforts were considered disrespectable; it says they were inconsistent with the hierarchical social structure and principles of the time, not that they were seen as disrespectable per se. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage states 'human society was a hierarchy of ranks in which every man was always engaged in being impressive towards his inferiors or respectful to those above him', directly matching this option. Choice D is incorrect — the passage says old furniture reflects physical habits of a class, not that owning old furniture was a sign of aristocracy.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E7_013","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P3","passage":"The invention of the means of being comfortable and the pursuit of comfort as a desirable end are modern phenomena, unparalleled in history since the time of the Romans. The padded-chain, the well spring bed, the sofa, central heating, and the regular hot bath and a host of other comforts enter into the daily lives of even the most moderately prosperous of the Anglo-Saxon bourgeoisie. Three hundred years ago, these were unknown to the greatest king.\\n\\nThe first thing that strikes one about the discomfort in which our ancestors lived is that it was mainly voluntary. Some of the apparatus of modern comfort is of purely modern invention; people could not put rubber tyres on their carriage before the discovery of rubber plant. But for the most part, there is nothing new about the material basis of our comfort. Men could have installed bathrooms and central heating and sanitary plumbing any time during the last three or four thousand years. Two thousand years before Christ, the inhabitants of Cnossos were familiar with sanitary plumbing. The Romans had invented an elaborate system of hot air heating, and the bathing facilities in a smart Roman villa were luxurious and completely beyond the dreams of modern man.\\n\\nIf the men of Middle Ages and early modern epoch lived in dirt and discomfort, it was not for any lack or ability to change their mode of life, it was because they chose to live in this way, because it fitted in with their principles and prejudices - political, moral, religious. What have comfort and cleanliness to do with politics, morals and religions? Let us begin with the consideration of armchair and heating.\\n\\nArmchairs and central heating became possible only with the breakdown of the power of kings, and great lords and the decay of social classes and the old family system. In the past, human society was a hierarchy of ranks in which every man was always engaged in being impressive towards his inferiors or respectful to those above him. Relaxing in armchair in such societies was utterly impossible. Old furniture reflects the physical habits of the class or society for which it was made.\\n\\nAnother essential component of modern comfort – the proper heating of houses – was made impossible for the great ones of the earth by the political structure of ancient societies. The great had to live in great palaces, with large and lofty halls in which they dined in state or received their guests. Splendid, but cold and drafty. Today, the self-made great men have not to keep up their positions in the splendid style of those who were born great, sacrificing grandeur to comfort, they live in rooms small enough to be heated. The third great comfort of modern age, the bath, is due, partly to the weakening of the tradition of religious asceticism which did not set much store by cleanliness. But it is principally to the doctors that bath-lovers owe their greatest debt. The discovery of infection through germs has put a premium on cleanliness. We wash now with religious fervour. Our baths have become something like magic rites to protect us from the powers of evil, embodied in the dirt-loving germ. Since the discovery of the beneficial effects of sunlight, too much clothing has become, medically speaking, a sin. And now comfort has not only become a physical habit but a fashion. But the achievement of comfort has been accompanied by a loss of other equally perhaps more valuable things. A man of means who builds a house today is in general concerned primarily with the comfort of his future residence. His ancient counterpart would have been primarily concerned with the impressiveness and magnificence of his dwelling – with beauty rather than comfort. Is our present passion for comfort a little exaggerated? Should not comfort be rightly regarded as a means to an end, as intended to create conditions which are favourable to the life of the mankind?","question":"What does the author comment regarding our passion for comfort today?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"We seem to be enamoured with the idea of making our life more and more comfortable.","B":"It is rather concerned with the beauty of things.","C":"Man is motivated by this passion to acquire many valuable things for comfort.","D":"Our passion for comfort fits perfectly with the principles and ethos of the present society."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"We seem to be enamoured with the idea of making our life more and more comfortable.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"contradicts_passage","C":"confuses_inference_with_assumption","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — the author asks 'Is our present passion for comfort a little exaggerated?' and notes comfort has become 'a physical habit but a fashion', implying we are obsessively focused on comfort; this matches 'enamoured with the idea'. Choice B is incorrect — the passage explicitly states today we prioritise comfort over beauty; ancient counterparts valued beauty first; saying we are 'concerned with the beauty of things' contradicts the passage. Choice C is incorrect — acquiring valuable things is described as a loss accompanying comfort, not a motivation for it; this confuses an effect with a cause. Choice D is incorrect — the author questions whether comfort has become too exaggerated, suggesting it does not perfectly fit with good principles; this is out of scope as stated.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E7_014","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P3","passage":"The invention of the means of being comfortable and the pursuit of comfort as a desirable end are modern phenomena, unparalleled in history since the time of the Romans. The padded-chain, the well spring bed, the sofa, central heating, and the regular hot bath and a host of other comforts enter into the daily lives of even the most moderately prosperous of the Anglo-Saxon bourgeoisie. Three hundred years ago, these were unknown to the greatest king.\\n\\nThe first thing that strikes one about the discomfort in which our ancestors lived is that it was mainly voluntary. Some of the apparatus of modern comfort is of purely modern invention; people could not put rubber tyres on their carriage before the discovery of rubber plant. But for the most part, there is nothing new about the material basis of our comfort. Men could have installed bathrooms and central heating and sanitary plumbing any time during the last three or four thousand years. Two thousand years before Christ, the inhabitants of Cnossos were familiar with sanitary plumbing. The Romans had invented an elaborate system of hot air heating, and the bathing facilities in a smart Roman villa were luxurious and completely beyond the dreams of modern man.\\n\\nIf the men of Middle Ages and early modern epoch lived in dirt and discomfort, it was not for any lack or ability to change their mode of life, it was because they chose to live in this way, because it fitted in with their principles and prejudices - political, moral, religious. What have comfort and cleanliness to do with politics, morals and religions? Let us begin with the consideration of armchair and heating.\\n\\nArmchairs and central heating became possible only with the breakdown of the power of kings, and great lords and the decay of social classes and the old family system. In the past, human society was a hierarchy of ranks in which every man was always engaged in being impressive towards his inferiors or respectful to those above him. Relaxing in armchair in such societies was utterly impossible. Old furniture reflects the physical habits of the class or society for which it was made.\\n\\nAnother essential component of modern comfort – the proper heating of houses – was made impossible for the great ones of the earth by the political structure of ancient societies. The great had to live in great palaces, with large and lofty halls in which they dined in state or received their guests. Splendid, but cold and drafty. Today, the self-made great men have not to keep up their positions in the splendid style of those who were born great, sacrificing grandeur to comfort, they live in rooms small enough to be heated. The third great comfort of modern age, the bath, is due, partly to the weakening of the tradition of religious asceticism which did not set much store by cleanliness. But it is principally to the doctors that bath-lovers owe their greatest debt. The discovery of infection through germs has put a premium on cleanliness. We wash now with religious fervour. Our baths have become something like magic rites to protect us from the powers of evil, embodied in the dirt-loving germ. Since the discovery of the beneficial effects of sunlight, too much clothing has become, medically speaking, a sin. And now comfort has not only become a physical habit but a fashion. But the achievement of comfort has been accompanied by a loss of other equally perhaps more valuable things. A man of means who builds a house today is in general concerned primarily with the comfort of his future residence. His ancient counterpart would have been primarily concerned with the impressiveness and magnificence of his dwelling – with beauty rather than comfort. Is our present passion for comfort a little exaggerated? Should not comfort be rightly regarded as a means to an end, as intended to create conditions which are favourable to the life of the mankind?","question":"What, according to the passage, is the reason why people in the Middle Ages lived in discomfort?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"They did not have the means to make their life comfortable.","B":"They were unable to change their mode of life.","C":"They lacked the knowledge to devise new methods to bring comfort into life.","D":"They chose such a way of life as it seemed to fit in with their principles."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"They chose such a way of life as it seemed to fit in with their principles.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"contradicts_passage","C":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage explicitly states they 'had the means'; their discomfort was voluntary, not due to lack of resources; this contradicts the passage. Choice B is incorrect — the passage states 'it was not for any lack or ability to change their mode of life'; they could have changed it but chose not to; this contradicts the passage. Choice C is incorrect — the passage states they had the knowledge (citing Cnossos, Romans); knowledge was available; this contradicts the passage. Choice D is CORRECT — the passage directly states 'they chose to live in this way, because it fitted in with their principles and prejudices — political, moral, religious.'","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E7_015","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P3","passage":"The invention of the means of being comfortable and the pursuit of comfort as a desirable end are modern phenomena, unparalleled in history since the time of the Romans. The padded-chain, the well spring bed, the sofa, central heating, and the regular hot bath and a host of other comforts enter into the daily lives of even the most moderately prosperous of the Anglo-Saxon bourgeoisie. Three hundred years ago, these were unknown to the greatest king.\\n\\nThe first thing that strikes one about the discomfort in which our ancestors lived is that it was mainly voluntary. Some of the apparatus of modern comfort is of purely modern invention; people could not put rubber tyres on their carriage before the discovery of rubber plant. But for the most part, there is nothing new about the material basis of our comfort. Men could have installed bathrooms and central heating and sanitary plumbing any time during the last three or four thousand years. Two thousand years before Christ, the inhabitants of Cnossos were familiar with sanitary plumbing. The Romans had invented an elaborate system of hot air heating, and the bathing facilities in a smart Roman villa were luxurious and completely beyond the dreams of modern man.\\n\\nIf the men of Middle Ages and early modern epoch lived in dirt and discomfort, it was not for any lack or ability to change their mode of life, it was because they chose to live in this way, because it fitted in with their principles and prejudices - political, moral, religious. What have comfort and cleanliness to do with politics, morals and religions? Let us begin with the consideration of armchair and heating.\\n\\nArmchairs and central heating became possible only with the breakdown of the power of kings, and great lords and the decay of social classes and the old family system. In the past, human society was a hierarchy of ranks in which every man was always engaged in being impressive towards his inferiors or respectful to those above him. Relaxing in armchair in such societies was utterly impossible. Old furniture reflects the physical habits of the class or society for which it was made.\\n\\nAnother essential component of modern comfort – the proper heating of houses – was made impossible for the great ones of the earth by the political structure of ancient societies. The great had to live in great palaces, with large and lofty halls in which they dined in state or received their guests. Splendid, but cold and drafty. Today, the self-made great men have not to keep up their positions in the splendid style of those who were born great, sacrificing grandeur to comfort, they live in rooms small enough to be heated. The third great comfort of modern age, the bath, is due, partly to the weakening of the tradition of religious asceticism which did not set much store by cleanliness. But it is principally to the doctors that bath-lovers owe their greatest debt. The discovery of infection through germs has put a premium on cleanliness. We wash now with religious fervour. Our baths have become something like magic rites to protect us from the powers of evil, embodied in the dirt-loving germ. Since the discovery of the beneficial effects of sunlight, too much clothing has become, medically speaking, a sin. And now comfort has not only become a physical habit but a fashion. But the achievement of comfort has been accompanied by a loss of other equally perhaps more valuable things. A man of means who builds a house today is in general concerned primarily with the comfort of his future residence. His ancient counterpart would have been primarily concerned with the impressiveness and magnificence of his dwelling – with beauty rather than comfort. Is our present passion for comfort a little exaggerated? Should not comfort be rightly regarded as a means to an end, as intended to create conditions which are favourable to the life of the mankind?","question":"Identify the statements that are true as per the passage: (a) Man seems to be trading off certain aspects of life in order to make his life more comfortable. (b) Most of the modern day inventions of comfort would have been possible if only our ancestors wished to lead an abstemious life. (c) Widespread prevalence of sofas and armchairs indicate an atrophy of stratification of society. (d) Certain actions like exposing our skin to sunlight, taking bath etc, seem to have sanction from the medical field.","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"a and b","B":"b, c and d","C":"a, c and d","D":"a, b, c and d"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"a, c and d","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"correct_but_unstated","D":"too_extreme"},"explanation":"Statement a is true — the passage states 'the achievement of comfort has been accompanied by a loss of other equally perhaps more valuable things', confirming a trade-off. Statement b is false — the passage says our ancestors chose discomfort because it fitted their principles, not because they wished to lead an abstemious (self-denying) life specifically; in fact, the passage says many comforts were available but voluntarily rejected for political/moral/religious reasons, not because of a desire for abstemiousness. Statement c is true — armchairs became possible with 'the breakdown of the power of kings… and the decay of social classes', meaning their prevalence signals the decline of hierarchical stratification. Statement d is true — the passage states doctors' discovery of germ infection put a premium on cleanliness, and sunlight's benefits are cited; both bathing and sun exposure have medical sanction. Therefore a, c and d — Choice C. Choice D is too extreme because it includes the false statement b.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E7_016","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P3","passage":"The invention of the means of being comfortable and the pursuit of comfort as a desirable end are modern phenomena, unparalleled in history since the time of the Romans. The padded-chain, the well spring bed, the sofa, central heating, and the regular hot bath and a host of other comforts enter into the daily lives of even the most moderately prosperous of the Anglo-Saxon bourgeoisie. Three hundred years ago, these were unknown to the greatest king.\\n\\nThe first thing that strikes one about the discomfort in which our ancestors lived is that it was mainly voluntary. Some of the apparatus of modern comfort is of purely modern invention; people could not put rubber tyres on their carriage before the discovery of rubber plant. But for the most part, there is nothing new about the material basis of our comfort. Men could have installed bathrooms and central heating and sanitary plumbing any time during the last three or four thousand years. Two thousand years before Christ, the inhabitants of Cnossos were familiar with sanitary plumbing. The Romans had invented an elaborate system of hot air heating, and the bathing facilities in a smart Roman villa were luxurious and completely beyond the dreams of modern man.\\n\\nIf the men of Middle Ages and early modern epoch lived in dirt and discomfort, it was not for any lack or ability to change their mode of life, it was because they chose to live in this way, because it fitted in with their principles and prejudices - political, moral, religious. What have comfort and cleanliness to do with politics, morals and religions? Let us begin with the consideration of armchair and heating.\\n\\nArmchairs and central heating became possible only with the breakdown of the power of kings, and great lords and the decay of social classes and the old family system. In the past, human society was a hierarchy of ranks in which every man was always engaged in being impressive towards his inferiors or respectful to those above him. Relaxing in armchair in such societies was utterly impossible. Old furniture reflects the physical habits of the class or society for which it was made.\\n\\nAnother essential component of modern comfort – the proper heating of houses – was made impossible for the great ones of the earth by the political structure of ancient societies. The great had to live in great palaces, with large and lofty halls in which they dined in state or received their guests. Splendid, but cold and drafty. Today, the self-made great men have not to keep up their positions in the splendid style of those who were born great, sacrificing grandeur to comfort, they live in rooms small enough to be heated. The third great comfort of modern age, the bath, is due, partly to the weakening of the tradition of religious asceticism which did not set much store by cleanliness. But it is principally to the doctors that bath-lovers owe their greatest debt. The discovery of infection through germs has put a premium on cleanliness. We wash now with religious fervour. Our baths have become something like magic rites to protect us from the powers of evil, embodied in the dirt-loving germ. Since the discovery of the beneficial effects of sunlight, too much clothing has become, medically speaking, a sin. And now comfort has not only become a physical habit but a fashion. But the achievement of comfort has been accompanied by a loss of other equally perhaps more valuable things. A man of means who builds a house today is in general concerned primarily with the comfort of his future residence. His ancient counterpart would have been primarily concerned with the impressiveness and magnificence of his dwelling – with beauty rather than comfort. Is our present passion for comfort a little exaggerated? Should not comfort be rightly regarded as a means to an end, as intended to create conditions which are favourable to the life of the mankind?","question":"Which of the following is true as inferred from the passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The sofa, the central heating system etc., were never used by the Romans.","B":"Only kings were used to the comforts of life.","C":"Comforts like sofa, central heating have become popular only in the last 300 years.","D":"The inventions of the means of being comfortable were not desirable to the Romans."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Comforts like sofa, central heating have become popular only in the last 300 years.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage states Romans had elaborate heating and bathing systems; saying they never used such comforts contradicts the passage. Choice B is incorrect — the passage states these comforts 'were unknown to the greatest king' three hundred years ago, meaning even kings lacked them; this contradicts the claim that only kings had comforts. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage states these comforts 'enter into the daily lives of even the most moderately prosperous' now, and 'three hundred years ago, these were unknown to the greatest king'; this is an inference that their modern widespread popularity is a recent phenomenon of the last 300 years. Choice D is incorrect — the Romans actively developed and enjoyed comfort (baths, heating); they were clearly desirable to Romans; this contradicts the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E7_017","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P3","passage":"The invention of the means of being comfortable and the pursuit of comfort as a desirable end are modern phenomena, unparalleled in history since the time of the Romans. The padded-chain, the well spring bed, the sofa, central heating, and the regular hot bath and a host of other comforts enter into the daily lives of even the most moderately prosperous of the Anglo-Saxon bourgeoisie. Three hundred years ago, these were unknown to the greatest king.\\n\\nThe first thing that strikes one about the discomfort in which our ancestors lived is that it was mainly voluntary. Some of the apparatus of modern comfort is of purely modern invention; people could not put rubber tyres on their carriage before the discovery of rubber plant. But for the most part, there is nothing new about the material basis of our comfort. Men could have installed bathrooms and central heating and sanitary plumbing any time during the last three or four thousand years. Two thousand years before Christ, the inhabitants of Cnossos were familiar with sanitary plumbing. The Romans had invented an elaborate system of hot air heating, and the bathing facilities in a smart Roman villa were luxurious and completely beyond the dreams of modern man.\\n\\nIf the men of Middle Ages and early modern epoch lived in dirt and discomfort, it was not for any lack or ability to change their mode of life, it was because they chose to live in this way, because it fitted in with their principles and prejudices - political, moral, religious. What have comfort and cleanliness to do with politics, morals and religions? Let us begin with the consideration of armchair and heating.\\n\\nArmchairs and central heating became possible only with the breakdown of the power of kings, and great lords and the decay of social classes and the old family system. In the past, human society was a hierarchy of ranks in which every man was always engaged in being impressive towards his inferiors or respectful to those above him. Relaxing in armchair in such societies was utterly impossible. Old furniture reflects the physical habits of the class or society for which it was made.\\n\\nAnother essential component of modern comfort – the proper heating of houses – was made impossible for the great ones of the earth by the political structure of ancient societies. The great had to live in great palaces, with large and lofty halls in which they dined in state or received their guests. Splendid, but cold and drafty. Today, the self-made great men have not to keep up their positions in the splendid style of those who were born great, sacrificing grandeur to comfort, they live in rooms small enough to be heated. The third great comfort of modern age, the bath, is due, partly to the weakening of the tradition of religious asceticism which did not set much store by cleanliness. But it is principally to the doctors that bath-lovers owe their greatest debt. The discovery of infection through germs has put a premium on cleanliness. We wash now with religious fervour. Our baths have become something like magic rites to protect us from the powers of evil, embodied in the dirt-loving germ. Since the discovery of the beneficial effects of sunlight, too much clothing has become, medically speaking, a sin. And now comfort has not only become a physical habit but a fashion. But the achievement of comfort has been accompanied by a loss of other equally perhaps more valuable things. A man of means who builds a house today is in general concerned primarily with the comfort of his future residence. His ancient counterpart would have been primarily concerned with the impressiveness and magnificence of his dwelling – with beauty rather than comfort. Is our present passion for comfort a little exaggerated? Should not comfort be rightly regarded as a means to an end, as intended to create conditions which are favourable to the life of the mankind?","question":"Which of the following is true about our ancestors?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The reason for most of their discomfort was that adequate resources may not have been available to them.","B":"Though they were familiar with the various ways of being comfortable, they chose not to make living conditions better.","C":"Central heating system and sanitary plumbing were considered uncomfortable by a particular section of people.","D":"None of them had the enthusiasm to discover resources to deal with their discomfort."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Though they were familiar with the various ways of being comfortable, they chose not to make living conditions better.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","C":"out_of_scope","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage explicitly states the opposite: 'it was not for any lack or ability to change their mode of life'; resources were available; this contradicts the passage. Choice B is CORRECT — the passage states our ancestors 'could have installed bathrooms and central heating' and that the inhabitants of Cnossos 'were familiar with sanitary plumbing', yet people of the Middle Ages chose discomfort; this matches the option exactly. Choice C is incorrect — the passage does not say anyone found these systems uncomfortable; the issue was principled choice, not physical discomfort with the systems themselves; this is out of scope. Choice D is incorrect — the Romans clearly had the enthusiasm to develop elaborate comfort systems; saying none of our ancestors did contradicts the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E7_018","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P4","passage":"Two representations have dominated public perceptions of the largest living marsupial carnivore, the Tasmanian devil. One is the voracious, hurricane-like, innocent savage, Taz, of Looney Tunes cartoon fame. The other, familiar in nineteenth-and twentieth-century rural Tasmania, is the ferocious predator and scavenger that wantonly kills livestock, and perhaps even people. Devils can take prey nearly three times their size and eat more than a third of their body weight in a sitting. Even so, it is hard to imagine how this species, being only slightly larger than a fox terrier, could be so maligned in name and image.\\n\\nIn Tasmanian Devil: A Unique and Threatened Animal, David Owen and David Pemberton delve into devil biology to convey the true nature of the beast once known to science as Sarcophilus satanicus. Fact and fiction are teased apart with sound science and tempered speculation. The devil's behaviour and physical appearance are explained in terms of its unique ecological position as a solitary, nocturnal predator that relies heavily on communal scavenging. Its larger cousin, the thylacine is now extinct, so the devil's present ecological interactions and selection pressures may differ somewhat from those under which it evolved. Although a useful starting point for those with an academic interest in the Tasmanian devil, this book has far wider appeal.\\n\\nThe humour and tragedy associated with early European settlers' misunderstanding of the devil are neatly woven together, and the author's arguments that the devil is not a rural menace are appealing. But I wonder whether the use of anecdotal evidence to lay the blame for poultry and trap raiding on the even rarer Spotted-tailed Quoll only extends the tyranny of prejudice.\\n\\nPeripheral connections to the devil story provide light relief. Particularly well fleshed out is the link between Theodore Flynn, who studied devil reproductive anatomy, his actor son Errol, who dubbed himself 'the Tasmanian devil', and Errol's employer, Warner Brothers, who have profited immensely from Taz cartoons and merchandising.\\n\\nThe inclusion of a wide array of reports and newspaper articles provides the reader with access to a mostly bygone mood of malevolence towards the devil, as well as to the voices that began to change this attitude. It is particularly sad that having survived being shot, poisoned, and trapped for bounties, and finally winning considerable public affection, devils are now succumbing to devil facial tumour disease. The authors relate the few clear facts about this hideous affliction, which seems to spread through biting and is devastating devil populations across much of Tasmania. It is unknown whether the disease is an old foe or whether its origins lie elsewhere, for example, in the accumulation of anthropogenic carcinogens.\\n\\nThe authors have succeeded in demystifying the Tasmanian devil and have revealed a fascinating creature; we would be much poorer without it.","question":"Which of the following statements about the Tasmanian Devil is true according to the passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The Tasmanian Devil is a voracious, hurricane-like, innocent savage.","B":"The Tasmanian Devil is both a nocturnal as well as a scavenging animal.","C":"It is difficult to imagine how much the name and the image of Tasmanian Devil has got maligned.","D":"Tasmanian Devils are the size of fox terriers."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"The Tasmanian Devil is both a nocturnal as well as a scavenging animal.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_inference_with_assumption","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — this is the Looney Tunes cartoon characterisation of Taz, described as a perception, not a factual description of the actual animal; the passage presents this as a misrepresentation, not a truth about the devil. Choice B is CORRECT — the passage explicitly states the devil is 'a solitary, nocturnal predator that relies heavily on communal scavenging', confirming it is both nocturnal and a scavenging animal. Choice C is incorrect — the passage says 'it is hard to imagine how this species… could be so maligned', meaning malignment is hard to understand given its modest size, not that imagining the degree of malignment is difficult; this misreads the sentence. Choice D is incorrect — the passage states the devil is 'only slightly larger than a fox terrier', not the same size; this contradicts the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E7_019","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P4","passage":"Two representations have dominated public perceptions of the largest living marsupial carnivore, the Tasmanian devil. One is the voracious, hurricane-like, innocent savage, Taz, of Looney Tunes cartoon fame. The other, familiar in nineteenth-and twentieth-century rural Tasmania, is the ferocious predator and scavenger that wantonly kills livestock, and perhaps even people. Devils can take prey nearly three times their size and eat more than a third of their body weight in a sitting. Even so, it is hard to imagine how this species, being only slightly larger than a fox terrier, could be so maligned in name and image.\\n\\nIn Tasmanian Devil: A Unique and Threatened Animal, David Owen and David Pemberton delve into devil biology to convey the true nature of the beast once known to science as Sarcophilus satanicus. Fact and fiction are teased apart with sound science and tempered speculation. The devil's behaviour and physical appearance are explained in terms of its unique ecological position as a solitary, nocturnal predator that relies heavily on communal scavenging. Its larger cousin, the thylacine is now extinct, so the devil's present ecological interactions and selection pressures may differ somewhat from those under which it evolved. Although a useful starting point for those with an academic interest in the Tasmanian devil, this book has far wider appeal.\\n\\nThe humour and tragedy associated with early European settlers' misunderstanding of the devil are neatly woven together, and the author's arguments that the devil is not a rural menace are appealing. But I wonder whether the use of anecdotal evidence to lay the blame for poultry and trap raiding on the even rarer Spotted-tailed Quoll only extends the tyranny of prejudice.\\n\\nPeripheral connections to the devil story provide light relief. Particularly well fleshed out is the link between Theodore Flynn, who studied devil reproductive anatomy, his actor son Errol, who dubbed himself 'the Tasmanian devil', and Errol's employer, Warner Brothers, who have profited immensely from Taz cartoons and merchandising.\\n\\nThe inclusion of a wide array of reports and newspaper articles provides the reader with access to a mostly bygone mood of malevolence towards the devil, as well as to the voices that began to change this attitude. It is particularly sad that having survived being shot, poisoned, and trapped for bounties, and finally winning considerable public affection, devils are now succumbing to devil facial tumour disease. The authors relate the few clear facts about this hideous affliction, which seems to spread through biting and is devastating devil populations across much of Tasmania. It is unknown whether the disease is an old foe or whether its origins lie elsewhere, for example, in the accumulation of anthropogenic carcinogens.\\n\\nThe authors have succeeded in demystifying the Tasmanian devil and have revealed a fascinating creature; we would be much poorer without it.","question":"Through the passage, the author mainly tries to","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"reveal the fascinating aspects of a book on the Tasmanian Devils.","B":"discuss the reasons behind the extinction of a species of animal.","C":"disclose the tyranny of prejudice against Tasmanian Devils.","D":"give a sympathetic report that demystifies the Tasmanian Devil."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"give a sympathetic report that demystifies the Tasmanian Devil.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"contradicts_passage","C":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — while the reviewer discusses the book, the passage's main purpose is to use the book review as a vehicle to convey sympathy for the Tasmanian devil and correct misconceptions; the book is a means, not the main point. Choice B is incorrect — the Tasmanian devil is not extinct; the passage explicitly discusses its current threats; saying the author discusses extinction contradicts the passage. Choice C is incorrect — while the tyranny of prejudice is mentioned as a concern in one paragraph, it is a subordinate point, not the main purpose; the overall sympathetic and demystifying mission is broader. Choice D is CORRECT — the reviewer's consistent tone is sympathetic (defending the devil against misconceptions, lamenting its current disease, calling it a 'fascinating creature'), and the passage explicitly states the authors have 'succeeded in demystifying the Tasmanian devil'.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E7_020","exercise":"exercise_7","passage_id":"RC_E7_P4","passage":"Two representations have dominated public perceptions of the largest living marsupial carnivore, the Tasmanian devil. One is the voracious, hurricane-like, innocent savage, Taz, of Looney Tunes cartoon fame. The other, familiar in nineteenth-and twentieth-century rural Tasmania, is the ferocious predator and scavenger that wantonly kills livestock, and perhaps even people. Devils can take prey nearly three times their size and eat more than a third of their body weight in a sitting. Even so, it is hard to imagine how this species, being only slightly larger than a fox terrier, could be so maligned in name and image.\\n\\nIn Tasmanian Devil: A Unique and Threatened Animal, David Owen and David Pemberton delve into devil biology to convey the true nature of the beast once known as Sarcophilus satanicus. Fact and fiction are teased apart with sound science and tempered speculation. The devil's behaviour and physical appearance are explained in terms of its unique ecological position as a solitary, nocturnal predator that relies heavily on communal scavenging. Its larger cousin, the thylacine is now extinct, so the devil's present ecological interactions and selection pressures may differ somewhat from those under which it evolved. Although a useful starting point for those with an academic interest in the Tasmanian devil, this book has far wider appeal.\\n\\nThe humour and tragedy associated with early European settlers' misunderstanding of the devil are neatly woven together, and the author's arguments that the devil is not a rural menace are appealing. But I wonder whether the use of anecdotal evidence to lay the blame for poultry and trap raiding on the even rarer Spotted-tailed Quoll only extends the tyranny of prejudice.\\n\\nPeripheral connections to the devil story provide light relief. Particularly well fleshed out is the link between Theodore Flynn, who studied devil reproductive anatomy, his actor son Errol, who dubbed himself 'the Tasmanian devil', and Errol's employer, Warner Brothers, who have profited immensely from Taz cartoons and merchandising.\\n\\nThe inclusion of a wide array of reports and newspaper articles provides the reader with access to a mostly bygone mood of malevolence towards the devil, as well as to the voices that began to change this attitude. It is particularly sad that having survived being shot, poisoned, and trapped for bounties, and finally winning considerable public affection, devils are now succumbing to devil facial tumour disease. The authors relate the few clear facts about this hideous affliction, which seems to spread through biting and is devastating devil populations across much of Tasmania. It is unknown whether the disease is an old foe or whether its origins lie elsewhere, for example, in the accumulation of anthropogenic carcinogens.\\n\\nThe authors have succeeded in demystifying the Tasmanian devil and have revealed a fascinating creature; we would be much poorer without it.","question":"Which one of the following is an inference from the passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Tasmanian Devils are found only in Tasmania.","B":"The reports on other related animals in the book by Owen and Pemberton are credible.","C":"The spreading of facial tumour disease can be curbed since Tasmanian devils are solitary nocturnal predators.","D":"Errol, son of Theodore Flynn, dubbed for the character Taz, of the Looney Tunes Cartoon."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Tasmanian Devils are found only in Tasmania.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"contradicts_passage","C":"confuses_inference_with_assumption","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — the passage never mentions Tasmanian devils existing anywhere outside Tasmania; combined with the name and all geographic references being Tasmanian, this can be inferred as their exclusive habitat. Choice B is incorrect — the passage explicitly casts doubt on anecdotal evidence used to blame the Spotted-tailed Quoll, suggesting the reviewer questions rather than endorses the credibility of such reports; this contradicts the option. Choice C is incorrect — the passage states the disease spreads through biting; being solitary at some times does not prevent transmission, as communal scavenging is also a behaviour; the logical leap that solitary behaviour would curb the disease is an unsupported assumption, not an inference from the passage. Choice D is incorrect — the passage states Errol 'dubbed himself the Tasmanian devil', meaning he gave himself the nickname, not that he voiced or dubbed the cartoon character Taz; this contradicts the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]`);export{e as default};
